[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate · 1:30] Great, thanks — let me play this back and then structure it. Brew & Bean, 200 US locations, specialty coffee, revenue up 15% over two years but net margin collapsing from 24% to 6%. That's an eighteen-point margin compression against a growing top line, which immediately tells me this isn't a demand problem — this is a cost structure problem, and almost certainly a fixed-cost or input-cost problem rather than a volume problem.

Here's how I'd structure it. Profit equals revenue minus cost. On the revenue side I'd decompose into number of stores, transactions per store, and average ticket — and critically, price versus mix, because in specialty coffee a chain can grow revenue 15% purely on unit expansion while same-store economics rot underneath. On the cost side I'd split COGS — green coffee, dairy, paper and packaging — from store-level operating costs, which is labor, rent, utilities, and then corporate overhead and marketing.

My hypothesis going in: if revenue is up 15% and they've been opening stores, the growth is store-count-driven, not comp-driven. Assuming same-store traffic is roughly flat to down 3% while new units dilute average unit volumes, you'd get exactly this pattern — revenue up, margin down — because each new store carries full rent and labor but lower throughput.

So my priority branch is cost, specifically the input and labor lines. Do we have a cost breakdown as a percentage of revenue for both periods? That'll tell us in thirty seconds where the eighteen points went.
