[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 15:42] Understood. Let's step away from the cost table now. Beyond pricing and procurement, what other levers could Brew and Bean pull to rebuild margin? Give me a structured set.

[candidate · 17:12] Sure. Beyond price and procurement there are four buckets, and I'd sequence them by speed to impact.

First, mix. You don't have to raise the price of a latte if you change what people buy. Food attach in specialty coffee typically runs 20 to 25% of transactions and carries materially higher gross margin than beverage; pushing attach up five points through bundling and register prompts is a margin lever with no headline price increase. Same logic on cold brew and specialty syrups, which carry better margins than drip.

Second, product and portion engineering. A 38% basket increase makes every gram of waste expensive. That's cup-size rationalization, lid and sleeve spec changes, dairy portioning discipline, and menu SKU pruning — fewer items means less spoilage and simpler prep. I'd expect two to three points recoverable here.

Third, store-level productivity. Labor's flat at 22%, which reads healthy, but flat isn't optimized — scheduling against hourly traffic curves, mobile order throughput, and equipment automation on espresso can hold labor while volume grows, which is deleverage in your favor.

Fourth, footprint and channel. Underperforming units should be culled or relocated at lease expiry, and I'd look hard at packaged retail and wholesale beans, which carry different economics entirely.

I'd want transaction volume and average ticket to size any of this properly — I asked for that earlier and never got it, so treat these as directional.
